namespace Ponca
{
  /*!
    \page ponca_points Defining points

    \tableofcontents

    \section ponca_points_intro Points in Ponca

    %Ponca is designed to be integrated in existing code bases. It therefore does not impose any data structure to
    store point clouds:
     - there is no point cloud class: algorithms receive points one at a time, or iterate over ranges of points
       (iterators, or indices in a random-access container),
     - the point type is a template parameter defined by the client code: it describes how the data of one sample
       (position, normal, ...) are accessed, and may simply wrap existing buffers,
     - no virtual function is involved: the computation performed for each neighbor is very small (a few sums), so the
       cost of dynamic dispatch would dominate. The requirements on the types are expressed as concepts instead, see
       \ref ponca_concepts.

    A point type must define (see \ref concepts_point for the complete list):
     - an integer `Dim`, the dimension of the ambient space (3 for 3D point clouds),
     - the types `Scalar`, `VectorType` and `MatrixType` used to represent scalars, vectors and matrices,
     - a default constructor,
     - read access to the position: `pos()`,
     - optionally, read access to a normal vector: `normal()`, required by the methods working with oriented points.

    A complete point type storing positions and normals in Eigen vectors is, for instance:

    \code
    struct MyPoint
    {
        enum { Dim = 3 };
        using Scalar     = float;
        using VectorType = Eigen::Matrix<Scalar, Dim, 1>;
        using MatrixType = Eigen::Matrix<Scalar, Dim, Dim>;

        PONCA_MULTIARCH inline MyPoint(const VectorType& pos    = VectorType::Zero(),
                                       const VectorType& normal = VectorType::Zero())
            : m_pos(pos), m_normal(normal) {}

        PONCA_MULTIARCH inline const VectorType& pos()    const { return m_pos; }
        PONCA_MULTIARCH inline const VectorType& normal() const { return m_normal; }

    private:
        VectorType m_pos, m_normal;
    };
    \endcode

    \note The `PONCA_MULTIARCH` macro marks the functions as callable from both the host and CUDA devices. It has no
    effect when compiling for the CPU only, see \ref ponca_gpu_page.

    \section points_utility Point types provided by Ponca

    The Common module (`#include <Ponca/Common>`, also included by `<Ponca/Fitting>`) provides point types that follow
    the point concept and rely on Eigen storage:

    | Class                                                | Content                       | Construction                                   |
    |------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------------|
    | Ponca::PointPosition<Scalar, Dim>                    | position                      | `PointPosition<float, 3> pt({1, 2, 3});`       |
    | Ponca::PointPositionNormal<Scalar, Dim>              | position and normal           | `PointPositionNormal<float, 3> pt({1, 2, 3}, {1, 0, 0});` |
    | Ponca::PointPositionNormalBinding<Scalar, Dim>       | read-only view on an interlaced buffer | `PointPositionNormalBinding<float, 3> pt(buffer, i);` |
    | Ponca::PointPositionNormalLazyBinding<Scalar, Dim>   | same, the view is created at each access | `PointPositionNormalLazyBinding<float, 3> pt(buffer, i);` |

    The binding types expect an interlaced buffer of scalars storing, for each point, its `Dim` coordinates followed by
    the `Dim` coordinates of its normal: the point of index `i` reads its position at `buffer + 2 * Dim * i` and its
    normal at `buffer + 2 * Dim * i + Dim`.
    - Ponca::PointPositionNormalBinding creates the `Eigen::Map` objects at construction: accessing the data is then
      free, but the object stores two maps.
    - Ponca::PointPositionNormalLazyBinding only stores the buffer pointer and the index: maps are created when
      `pos()` or `normal()` are called. Its `bind(buffer)` function changes the buffer, e.g. to process successive
      frames of an animation with the same points.

    \section ponca_points_binding Using existing data without copy

    Since points are only accessed through `pos()` and `normal()`, a point type can be a lightweight view over data
    stored elsewhere. In the following, the binding type is created on the fly for every neighbor, and no data is
    duplicated:

    \code
    using MyPoint = Ponca::PointPositionNormalBinding<double, 3>;
    using Filter  = Ponca::DistWeightFilter<MyPoint, Ponca::SmoothWeightKernel<double>>;
    using Fit     = Ponca::Basket<MyPoint, Filter, Ponca::OrientedSphereFit, Ponca::GLSParam>;

    // buffer: 6 * n doubles (x, y, z, nx, ny, nz for each point), owned by the application
    Fit fit;
    fit.setNeighborFilter({evaluationPoint, scale});
    fit.init();
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)
        fit.addNeighbor(MyPoint(buffer, i));
    fit.finalize();
    \endcode

    The complete program is given in \ref example_cxx_binding_page.

    \section ponca_points_containers Collections of points

    There is no notion of point set in %Ponca: the functions that process several points take ranges.
    - Fitting objects iterate over a container or a pair of iterators (`compute`), or over a range of indices in a
      random-access container (`computeWithIds`), see \ref fitting_Fitting.
    - Spatial data structures (Ponca::KdTreeDense, Ponca::KdTreeSparse) copy the points they are built from, or convert
      them with a user-provided functor, and give access to them with `points()`. Ponca::KnnGraph uses the points of
      the kd-tree it is built from. See \ref spatialpartitioning.

    While any container providing these accesses can be used, a contiguous container (`std::vector`, a raw buffer, ...)
    is recommended for performance.

    \section ponca_points_generation Generating synthetic points

    The Common module also provides functions that sample simple shapes, mostly used to test the library and to write
    examples. Positions and normals are perturbed by default; the noise amplitude is absolute (it does not depend on
    the size of the shape).

    | Function                                                    | Samples                                                                       |
    |-------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
    | Ponca::getPointOnSphere<DataPoint>(radius, center, addPositionNoise = true, addNormalNoise = true, reverseNormals = false) | a uniformly distributed point of the sphere, with an outward normal. The position noise is a random displacement of norm at most 0.01; the normal noise recomputes the normal from such a displaced position; `reverseNormals` flips each normal with probability 1/2 |
    | Ponca::getPointOnPlane<DataPoint>(position, normal, radius, addPositionNoise = true, addNormalNoise = true, reverseNormals = false) | a point of the plane at distance `radius` from `position`, in a random direction. The normal noise rotates the normal by up to \f$\pi/16\f$ around two tangent axes |
    | Ponca::getPointOnRectangularPlane<DataPoint>(position, normal, width, height, xAxis, yAxis, addPositionNoise = true) | a point of the rectangle `position + u * xAxis + v * yAxis`, \f$|u| \leq\f$ `width/2`, \f$|v| \leq\f$ `height/2` (positions only, `normal` is ignored) |
    | Ponca::getPointOnCircle<VectorType>(radius, center)         | a uniformly distributed position in the disk of the xy-plane (returns a `VectorType`, only `x` and `y` are modified) |
    | Ponca::getPointOnParaboloid<DataPoint>(a, b, s, addNoise = true) | a point of the paraboloid \f$z = a x^2 + b y^2\f$, \f$(x, y) \in [-s, s]^2\f$, with a normal (3D only) |
    | Ponca::getPointOnParaboloid<DataPoint>(a, b, c, d, e, f, s, addNoise = true) | a point of \f$z = a x^2 + b y^2 + c x y + d x + e y + f\f$, \f$(x, y)\f$ in the disk of radius `s` (3D only; normals are set when the point type has normals) |
    | Ponca::getRandomPoint<DataPoint>()                           | a point at distance \f$[0.99, 1.01]\f$ from the origin in a random direction, with a normal close to this direction |

    \warning These functions rely on `Eigen::internal::random`, i.e. on `std::rand()`: seed it with `std::srand` to
    get reproducible samples. The sequence differs between platforms.
  */
} // End of Ponca namespace
